1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,030
Imagine being a web developer
in the early two thousands.

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:07,270
You have written a perfectly
reasonable web page.

3
00:00:08,199 --> 00:00:11,109
It works in Netscape. It works in Opera.

4
00:00:11,759 --> 00:00:13,169
It works in the new Firefox.

5
00:00:14,139 --> 00:00:18,289
And then you open it in Internet
Explorer. Everything is broken.

6
00:00:19,269 --> 00:00:22,539
The layout is wrong.
The buttons have moved.

7
00:00:23,429 --> 00:00:25,419
The PNG images look terrible.

8
00:00:26,249 --> 00:00:30,979
And somehow, adding one line break to
your HTML has created a three pixel gap.

9
00:00:31,765 --> 00:00:35,485
Welcome to web development in
the age of Internet Explorer six.

10
00:00:35,645 --> 00:00:38,175
But here's the interesting part.

11
00:00:39,225 --> 00:00:42,095
Internet Explorer six wasn't
simply a terrible browser.

12
00:00:43,065 --> 00:00:46,795
Microsoft was trying to solve
problems that the web genuinely had.

13
00:00:47,305 --> 00:00:50,515
They just had one enormous advantage
over everyone else. Windows.

14
00:00:52,507 --> 00:00:55,667
And eventually, that advantage
allowed Microsoft to turn

15
00:00:55,747 --> 00:00:58,337
the browser into something
much bigger than a browser.

16
00:01:01,437 --> 00:01:06,107
In the mid nineteen nineties, the web
was dominated by Netscape Navigator.

17
00:01:06,977 --> 00:01:09,247
Microsoft wasn't happy about that.

18
00:01:09,941 --> 00:01:14,091
So Internet Explorer was bundled with
Windows and made available for free.

19
00:01:14,961 --> 00:01:16,351
You bought a Windows computer.

20
00:01:17,021 --> 00:01:23,431
Internet Explorer was already there.
Nothing to download. Nothing to pay for.

21
00:01:24,103 --> 00:01:28,053
And most importantly, Microsoft
controlled the operating system.

22
00:01:28,983 --> 00:01:32,023
By the early two thousands,
Internet Explorer had

23
00:01:32,103 --> 00:01:34,633
more than ninety percent
of the browser market.

24
00:01:35,423 --> 00:01:37,463
Internet Explorer six arrived in two

25
00:01:37,543 --> 00:01:40,373
thousand and one,
together with Windows XP.

26
00:01:41,403 --> 00:01:45,253
And for a while, it looked like Microsoft
had simply won the browser wars.

27
00:01:47,263 --> 00:01:48,733
Microsoft had a problem.

28
00:01:49,343 --> 00:01:51,213
The web was extremely limited.

29
00:01:52,383 --> 00:01:53,993
HTML could display documents.

30
00:01:54,863 --> 00:01:56,633
CSS could style them.

31
00:01:57,563 --> 00:01:59,253
JavaScript could make them interactive.

32
00:02:00,252 --> 00:02:01,722
But that was about it.

33
00:02:02,492 --> 00:02:05,982
Anything more complicated ran straight
into the limits of the browser.

34
00:02:07,006 --> 00:02:08,916
What if you wanted to scan a document?

35
00:02:09,706 --> 00:02:11,716
Or communicate with a smart card?

36
00:02:12,406 --> 00:02:14,636
Or talk to some piece of Windows hardware?

37
00:02:15,554 --> 00:02:17,744
The browser couldn't
really do those things.

38
00:02:17,874 --> 00:02:22,184
So Microsoft came up
with a solution. ActiveX.

39
00:02:23,078 --> 00:02:24,948
The basic idea was simple.

40
00:02:25,818 --> 00:02:29,068
Why restrict a web application
to what the browser can do?

41
00:02:29,598 --> 00:02:31,888
Just let it use native Windows components.

42
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:38,080
And suddenly a web page could do things
that ordinary HTML couldn't even dream of.

43
00:02:40,330 --> 00:02:44,540
Today, ActiveX sounds
terrifying. And for good reason.

44
00:02:45,570 --> 00:02:48,070
An ActiveX component
could reach the files, the

45
00:02:48,150 --> 00:02:51,460
applications, the hardware
and the operating system.

46
00:02:52,121 --> 00:02:55,931
From today's security perspective,
that sounds absolutely insane.

47
00:02:57,301 --> 00:03:01,611
But in the early two thousands, it made
perfect sense for corporate applications.

48
00:03:02,672 --> 00:03:05,102
Companies didn't want a
pretty document viewer.

49
00:03:05,892 --> 00:03:07,262
They wanted applications.

50
00:03:08,012 --> 00:03:12,182
They wanted web pages that could
behave almost like desktop software.

51
00:03:13,012 --> 00:03:15,562
And Microsoft gave them exactly that.

52
00:03:16,292 --> 00:03:19,872
Even Windows Update itself
originally relied on ActiveX to

53
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:23,482
inspect the system and work out
which updates were required.

54
00:03:24,395 --> 00:03:27,465
So ActiveX wasn't simply a stupid mistake.

55
00:03:28,415 --> 00:03:32,045
It was an early attempt to turn the
browser into an application platform.

56
00:03:32,877 --> 00:03:36,167
And in that sense, Microsoft
was actually ahead of its time.

57
00:03:36,997 --> 00:03:39,807
The problem was how much
power they gave that platform.

58
00:03:42,737 --> 00:03:45,367
ActiveX wasn't the only
Microsoft extension.

59
00:03:45,917 --> 00:03:49,107
There was VBScript. There was DHTML.

60
00:03:49,557 --> 00:03:53,187
There was even CSS expression. Yes.

61
00:03:54,103 --> 00:03:58,353
Internet Explorer could evaluate
JavaScript directly inside CSS.

62
00:03:59,183 --> 00:04:00,993
You could do something like this.

63
00:04:03,623 --> 00:04:08,193
It was ridiculous. It was also
sometimes surprisingly useful.

64
00:04:08,943 --> 00:04:11,593
And none of it was guaranteed
to work anywhere else.

65
00:04:12,385 --> 00:04:15,015
Microsoft wasn't simply
implementing the web.

66
00:04:15,665 --> 00:04:18,795
Microsoft was extending the
web in its own direction.

67
00:04:19,205 --> 00:04:23,535
And with ninety percent of the market,
it had enough power to make that work.

68
00:04:26,215 --> 00:04:29,845
One of the most famous
examples was the CSS box model.

69
00:04:30,695 --> 00:04:33,675
The standard model said that if
an element was two hundred pixels

70
00:04:33,755 --> 00:04:37,845
wide, with twenty pixels of
padding and a five pixel border,

71
00:04:38,601 --> 00:04:41,591
then the total width was two
hundred and fifty pixels.

72
00:04:42,537 --> 00:04:45,627
Older versions of Internet
Explorer read it differently.

73
00:04:46,817 --> 00:04:50,627
In quirks mode, the declared width could
include the padding and the border.

74
00:04:51,445 --> 00:04:54,715
This was not necessarily an
insane way to think about width.

75
00:04:55,685 --> 00:04:59,555
In fact, today we often deliberately
use exactly this kind of behaviour.

76
00:05:00,545 --> 00:05:02,475
It is called border box sizing.

77
00:05:03,321 --> 00:05:06,571
The real problem was that different
browsers behaved differently.

78
00:05:07,481 --> 00:05:10,991
And that meant the developer
had to care. A lot.

79
00:05:12,621 --> 00:05:14,761
There was something called hasLayout.

80
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:17,531
It wasn't a normal CSS property.

81
00:05:18,341 --> 00:05:20,031
It wasn't part of the standard.

82
00:05:20,781 --> 00:05:23,891
But it decided how Internet
Explorer laid out elements.

83
00:05:24,819 --> 00:05:26,539
And sometimes a broken layout could

84
00:05:26,619 --> 00:05:32,349
mysteriously be fixed by adding
a single line. Zoom one. Why?

85
00:05:32,859 --> 00:05:36,289
Because zoom one could trigger
the internal hasLayout mechanism.

86
00:05:37,231 --> 00:05:39,621
There were problems with PNG transparency.

87
00:05:40,811 --> 00:05:43,991
There were problems with select
elements appearing above absolutely

88
00:05:44,071 --> 00:05:47,921
positioned popups, regardless
of how high you set the z-index.

89
00:05:49,254 --> 00:05:52,664
Web development wasn't just
about implementing your design.

90
00:05:53,414 --> 00:05:56,544
It was about learning the bugs
of a particular browser engine.

91
00:05:59,404 --> 00:06:00,944
Here's another wonderful example.

92
00:06:01,024 --> 00:06:02,914
Whitespace.

93
00:06:04,104 --> 00:06:06,874
And this one wasn't even
Internet Explorer's fault.

94
00:06:07,424 --> 00:06:10,104
You could put two images right
next to each other in the

95
00:06:10,184 --> 00:06:13,214
markup, with nothing between
the tags, and get one result.

96
00:06:14,162 --> 00:06:18,732
Then someone would nicely format the
HTML and put each tag on its own line.

97
00:06:19,462 --> 00:06:22,612
And suddenly there was a few
pixel gap between the images.

98
00:06:23,702 --> 00:06:25,472
The newline became significant.

99
00:06:26,443 --> 00:06:29,273
Your code formatter could
literally break your web page.

100
00:06:30,103 --> 00:06:31,763
And if you were using
an old version of Visual

101
00:06:31,843 --> 00:06:35,573
Studio, it might happily
format your HTML for you.

102
00:06:36,263 --> 00:06:37,633
Thank you, Microsoft.

103
00:06:39,603 --> 00:06:45,193
Developers accumulated an entire toolbox
of workarounds. Conditional comments.

104
00:06:47,493 --> 00:06:50,853
And sometimes completely
bizarre looking HTML whose

105
00:06:50,933 --> 00:06:54,063
only purpose was to make
Internet Explorer behave.

106
00:06:54,949 --> 00:06:57,979
You didn't write ugly code
because you were a bad programmer.

107
00:06:58,389 --> 00:07:01,879
You wrote ugly code because
the browser forced you to.

108
00:07:04,369 --> 00:07:07,289
Internet Explorer also
crashed spectacularly,

109
00:07:07,369 --> 00:07:09,979
and often took every open window with it.

110
00:07:10,652 --> 00:07:13,362
Which led to a wonderful
category of office entertainment.

111
00:07:14,632 --> 00:07:16,212
Hey, check out this link.

112
00:07:16,292 --> 00:07:19,442
And twenty Internet
Explorer windows disappear.

113
00:07:20,416 --> 00:07:23,246
This was the web before
browsers were sandboxed.

114
00:07:23,836 --> 00:07:25,426
Today's browser says this.

115
00:07:25,936 --> 00:07:29,626
This website is untrusted.
I'll isolate it.

116
00:07:30,289 --> 00:07:33,219
Internet Explorer six
sometimes felt more like this.

117
00:07:34,149 --> 00:07:36,309
You're a web page? Sure.

118
00:07:36,389 --> 00:07:37,839
Here's the keys to the building.

119
00:07:40,409 --> 00:07:44,959
Why did developers actually use
these hacks? Because they had to.

120
00:07:45,733 --> 00:07:48,583
Imagine a company with
five hundred employees.

121
00:07:49,293 --> 00:07:51,323
Every computer runs Windows XP.

122
00:07:51,953 --> 00:07:54,523
Every computer has Internet Explorer.

123
00:07:55,305 --> 00:07:58,825
The developer has two options. Option one.

124
00:07:58,945 --> 00:08:02,655
Build for Internet Explorer,
which everyone already has.

125
00:08:03,427 --> 00:08:08,127
Option two. Build for every browser,
test everything everywhere, and

126
00:08:08,207 --> 00:08:11,897
explain to the IT department
why everyone needs a second one.

127
00:08:12,727 --> 00:08:14,417
The answer was usually obvious.

128
00:08:15,388 --> 00:08:18,958
They weren't choosing Microsoft
technology because they loved Microsoft.

129
00:08:19,648 --> 00:08:22,538
They were choosing what
solved the customer's problem.

130
00:08:23,288 --> 00:08:26,218
And sometimes ActiveX was
the best tool available.

131
00:08:29,088 --> 00:08:31,018
And eventually someone had enough.

132
00:08:31,888 --> 00:08:35,158
JavaScript libraries started
hiding the browser differences.

133
00:08:36,108 --> 00:08:38,058
One of the most important was jQuery.

134
00:08:39,172 --> 00:08:42,122
Instead of writing browser
specific event handling,

135
00:08:42,332 --> 00:08:44,822
you could write a single
line that worked everywhere.

136
00:08:45,711 --> 00:08:47,691
Instead of manually
dealing with different AJAX

137
00:08:47,771 --> 00:08:51,261
implementations, you could
use a common abstraction.

138
00:08:52,271 --> 00:08:55,281
jQuery didn't make Internet
Explorer disappear.

139
00:08:55,651 --> 00:08:59,061
But it made it much easier
to pretend it didn't exist.

140
00:09:00,051 --> 00:09:03,261
And for web developers of that
era, that was a beautiful thing.

141
00:09:06,011 --> 00:09:07,401
Firefox appeared.

142
00:09:07,711 --> 00:09:09,801
Opera continued developing.

143
00:09:10,331 --> 00:09:11,271
Safari arrived.

144
00:09:11,351 --> 00:09:13,521
And eventually Google released Chrome.

145
00:09:14,367 --> 00:09:18,317
The browser wars were back, and this
time the competition was different.

146
00:09:18,707 --> 00:09:21,387
JavaScript engines became
dramatically better.

147
00:09:21,467 --> 00:09:24,417
HTML five arrived.

148
00:09:25,411 --> 00:09:27,811
The browser itself started
learning all the things

149
00:09:27,891 --> 00:09:30,881
developers had previously
needed plugins for.

150
00:09:31,565 --> 00:09:36,065
Video. Audio. Canvas.
SVG. WebGL. WebSockets.

151
00:09:36,145 --> 00:09:38,095
And eventually WebRTC.

152
00:09:38,605 --> 00:09:41,495
The web was becoming an
application platform.

153
00:09:42,332 --> 00:09:46,262
Which was exactly what Microsoft had
been trying to achieve with ActiveX.

154
00:09:46,872 --> 00:09:49,502
But now it was happening
through open standards.

155
00:09:52,352 --> 00:09:54,362
Microsoft did try once more.

156
00:09:55,152 --> 00:09:57,212
Silverlight let developers
build rich browser

157
00:09:57,292 --> 00:10:00,202
applications using C sharp and dot NET.

158
00:10:01,112 --> 00:10:03,462
It even powered Netflix's early streaming.

159
00:10:04,249 --> 00:10:06,199
But the web didn't want plugins forever.

160
00:10:07,349 --> 00:10:09,799
Apple refused to support
Flash on the iPhone.

161
00:10:10,369 --> 00:10:15,339
And suddenly the future wasn't this.
Browser plus plugin. It was this.

162
00:10:15,769 --> 00:10:16,969
The browser itself.

163
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:20,820
Silverlight disappeared.
Flash disappeared.

164
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:23,210
ActiveX disappeared.

165
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,810
And Internet Explorer
eventually disappeared too.

166
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:31,810
And this is where the
story gets interesting.

167
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:35,150
Because Microsoft wasn't completely wrong.

168
00:10:35,790 --> 00:10:37,520
They had correctly predicted that the

169
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:40,050
browser would become an
application platform.

170
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:43,770
They were just trying to get there
through proprietary technologies.

171
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:47,690
The web reached the same
destination through standards.

172
00:10:48,575 --> 00:10:52,295
The browser won. But it won
by becoming something far more

173
00:10:52,375 --> 00:10:54,085
powerful than the browser
of two thousand and one.

174
00:10:57,399 --> 00:11:00,269
So was Microsoft the villain? Not really.

175
00:11:01,019 --> 00:11:03,269
And that's what makes the
story more interesting.

176
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:06,410
Microsoft had brilliant engineers.

177
00:11:07,340 --> 00:11:09,150
They had enormous resources.

178
00:11:09,260 --> 00:11:13,330
And they built many technologies that
were genuinely ahead of their time.

179
00:11:14,025 --> 00:11:17,355
But Microsoft also had an
overwhelming market position.

180
00:11:18,305 --> 00:11:20,475
And that created a dangerous incentive.

181
00:11:21,505 --> 00:11:24,715
If almost everyone uses your
browser, why wait for standards?

182
00:11:24,865 --> 00:11:27,955
Why not simply implement
your own solution?

183
00:11:28,165 --> 00:11:30,115
For a while, that worked.

184
00:11:30,525 --> 00:11:32,635
Until the rest of the web caught up.

185
00:11:35,305 --> 00:11:37,355
Today, Internet Explorer is gone.

186
00:11:37,985 --> 00:11:40,335
The old browser wars seem
like ancient history.

187
00:11:41,005 --> 00:11:46,655
But look at the modern browser market.
Chrome. Edge. Opera. Brave. Vivaldi.

188
00:11:47,481 --> 00:11:51,091
And many others are based on
Chromium. Different products.

189
00:11:52,081 --> 00:11:54,431
Often the same underlying browser engine.

190
00:11:55,261 --> 00:11:56,991
So perhaps we've traded this.

191
00:11:57,821 --> 00:12:01,051
One browser everyone had
to support. For this.

192
00:12:01,701 --> 00:12:05,591
One engine everyone builds
on. Does that sound familiar?

193
00:12:06,501 --> 00:12:10,871
Because a dominant browser engine doesn't
just decide how websites are displayed.

194
00:12:11,739 --> 00:12:13,909
It influences what developers build.

195
00:12:14,059 --> 00:12:16,529
Which technologies become popular.

196
00:12:16,659 --> 00:12:19,649
And ultimately, what
the web itself becomes.

197
00:12:22,629 --> 00:12:25,099
There is, however, one
very important difference.

198
00:12:25,929 --> 00:12:28,479
Today's web is built
around open standards.

199
00:12:29,389 --> 00:12:31,519
Firefox still has its own engine.

200
00:12:32,249 --> 00:12:33,679
Safari has WebKit.

201
00:12:34,349 --> 00:12:35,859
Chromium is open source.

202
00:12:36,706 --> 00:12:39,196
And modern browsers update automatically.

203
00:12:40,086 --> 00:12:42,036
We are nowhere near the situation
of two thousand and three.

204
00:12:43,844 --> 00:12:45,494
But the basic question remains.

205
00:12:46,504 --> 00:12:50,954
How much influence should one company have
over the platform used by almost everyone?

206
00:12:51,424 --> 00:12:54,114
That's the question that
never really went away.

207
00:12:56,994 --> 00:13:00,994
So, was Internet Explorer
six terrible? Yes.

208
00:13:01,074 --> 00:13:02,924
Sometimes spectacularly terrible.

209
00:13:03,793 --> 00:13:05,543
But that's not the whole story.

210
00:13:06,113 --> 00:13:07,983
It was also a product of its time.

211
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:10,940
The problem was that
Microsoft had won the browser

212
00:13:11,020 --> 00:13:14,570
wars so completely that
it could define the rules.

213
00:13:15,421 --> 00:13:17,351
And web developers paid the price.

214
00:13:18,181 --> 00:13:20,321
They learned the mysteries of hasLayout.

215
00:13:20,661 --> 00:13:25,251
They discovered that zoom one could
magically fix things. And they prayed.

216
00:13:26,147 --> 00:13:30,857
And eventually, Internet Explorer became
what it had spent twenty years creating.

217
00:13:31,927 --> 00:13:34,997
A legacy platform that nobody
wanted to support anymore.

218
00:13:35,845 --> 00:13:38,475
The web didn't reject Microsoft's vision.

219
00:13:39,245 --> 00:13:41,415
It simply built a better version of it.

220
00:13:42,205 --> 00:13:45,215
And if there's one thing that
era should teach us, it's this.

221
00:13:46,036 --> 00:13:49,446
Technology doesn't become a
standard just because it's good.

222
00:13:50,185 --> 00:13:52,645
Sometimes it becomes
a standard because one

223
00:13:52,725 --> 00:13:55,655
company has enough power
to make everyone use it.

224
00:13:56,276 --> 00:13:58,386
And sometimes, twenty years later...

225
00:13:58,976 --> 00:14:00,566
we realise we've done it again.
